---
<BaseLayout
  title="The Switch We Never Chose | James Tannahill"
  description={description}
  canonical={url}
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="https://jamestannahill.com/og-the-switch-we-never-chose.png?v=1"
  ogImageAlt="The Switch We Never Chose - on the light we keep on from sundown to sleep"
  publishedTime={published}
  articleSection="Essays"
  extraSchema={schema}
>
  <div class="thoughts-page min-h-screen">
    <article class="max-w-[728px] mx-auto px-6 py-16">

      <EssayHeader slug="the-switch-we-never-chose" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> We keep every room bright from sunset until bedtime out of habit, not choice, and we do the same to our minds with screens. It is worth choosing both on purpose.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The sun goes down, and before I have made any decision at all, my hand is already on the wall.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">I have started catching myself doing it. Around dusk the room begins to gray, and a quiet reflex fires: fix this. Flip the switch. Restore the day. It does not feel like a choice. It feels like breathing.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Lately I have been leaving my hand at my side. I let the room go blue, then gray, then dark, and I sit there. The strange part is not how uncomfortable it is. It is how good it is. The edges of things soften. My shoulders drop. The evening gets longer, not shorter. After a while I can see again, just differently.</p>
        <p>I like the dark, and I have come to think there is more to that than preference. Somewhere along the way, society made the lightbulb normal and then made it necessary. We light our homes from sundown until our eyes close, and nobody remembers agreeing to it.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
        <img
          src="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-09-26-060044/australasia-60s-100e/preview-1200.webp"
          width="1200"
          height="1200"
          alt="Abstract painting of a pale band of light and scattered points drifting diagonally across a deep blue-black ground, like the Milky Way over a dark sky"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          class="w-full h-auto t-plate"
        />
        <figcaption class="e-cap mt-3">
          60S 100E, Southern Ocean below Australia, rendered from that day's atmospheric data.
          From <a href="https://art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-09-26-060044/australasia-60s-100e/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="underline hover:opacity-70 break-all">art.jamestannahill.com/weather/2026-09-26-060044/australasia-60s-100e</a>.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Night Used to Be Night</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">For almost all of human history, the dark was simply what happened when the sun left. Light after sundown was expensive. Candles and tallow cost real money, and fire cost real effort. So people mostly did not fight the night. They worked with it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The historian Roger Ekirch spent years in diaries, court records and letters from pre-industrial Europe and found something odd. People casually mentioned a "first sleep" and a "second sleep." They went to bed not long after dark, woke for an hour or so in the middle of the night, and then slept again. That waking hour was quiet time. People prayed, talked, made love, lay there thinking. It was so ordinary that nobody bothered to explain it.</p>
        <p>Then the lights came on. Gas lamps spread through cities in the early 1800s, and Edison's bulb arrived in 1879. Within a few generations, electricity reached nearly every home in the industrialized world. The economist William Nordhaus calculated that the amount of work needed to buy a fixed amount of light fell by a factor in the tens of thousands between 1800 and the late twentieth century. Something that shaped human life for hundreds of thousands of years was overwritten in about five generations. The arrangement we treat as permanent is brand new.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Why We All Go Along</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Nobody voted on this. No one sat us down and explained why every room must be bright until bedtime. So why does almost everyone do it? I see six reasons.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>It got cheap.</strong> When light cost something, darkness was the default and light was a decision. Once the cost disappeared, the default flipped. Now darkness is the decision, and most of us never make it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Work wanted it.</strong> Light stretched the working day past sunset, and factories ran shifts. Darkness stopped meaning rest and started meaning idleness, and idleness became a moral failing.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>We are afraid.</strong> Some of that fear is ancient and earned, because things in the dark used to eat us. Some of it was taught. Street lighting was sold as civic order, as safety, as civilization itself.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Light signals that all is well.</strong> A lit window says someone is home. A dark house says something is wrong: nobody is there, or someone is sick, or broke, or sad. We keep the lights on partly for the neighbors.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Our language already picked a side.</strong> The Enlightenment. The Dark Ages. A bright future. A dark mood. Before we reach for the switch, our words have told us which one is good.</p>
        <p><strong>Inertia.</strong> Mostly, we just do what the house we grew up in did.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">We Did Not Buy Light. We Took a Job.</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">This is the part that bothers me most. We think of the lightbulb as something we own, but look at what it asks of us.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Every evening we <strong>act</strong>: we flip the switch, room by room, without thinking. We <strong>watch</strong>: some small part of our attention is always on duty, noticing the flicker in the hallway or the dead bulb over the stove. We <strong>maintain</strong>: we buy bulbs, store spares, match the color temperature, drag out the ladder, and work out how to throw the old ones away. And we <strong>design</strong>: entire professions, budgets and fixture plans exist to make sure no space anywhere is ever dark.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Some of this is not even optional. Building codes in most American jurisdictions, following the National Electrical Code, require a switched light or switched outlet in every habitable room, and switched lighting in bathrooms, hallways and stairways. In most places you cannot legally build a dark house. Darkness has been written out of the building code.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Some of it was engineered. In 1924 the world's major bulb makers formed what became known as the Phoebus cartel and agreed to limit incandescent bulbs to about a thousand hours of life, shorter than many bulbs already lasted. The cycle of burning out and replacing that we live inside was, in part, a business plan.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Underneath all of it, darkness itself came to be treated as a malfunction. A dark room is a problem to solve, and a power cut is an emergency. We have no ordinary word for a room that is dark on purpose and perfectly fine.</p>
        <p>Thoreau saw it coming at Walden: "men have become the tools of their tools." We bought light, and in return we signed up to maintain it every night for the rest of our lives.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Other Lightbulb</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The longer I sit in the dark, the more I notice that the room is not the only thing I keep lit.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">My mind gets the same treatment. Every gap gets filled. A podcast for the dishes. A feed in the checkout line. Something on in the background so the apartment is not quiet. The phone is the newest lightbulb, and we hold it six inches from our faces right up until our eyes close. "Lit from sundown to eyes shut" began as a description of the room. It describes the head too.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The obligations carry over almost exactly. We <strong>act</strong>: we reach for the phone in every pause. We <strong>watch</strong>: badges, unread counts, the buzz in the pocket. We <strong>maintain</strong>: we charge, update, clear the inbox and manage the subscriptions. We <strong>design</strong>: we curate the feeds, arrange the home screen and build systems for productivity. It is the same job, done a second time inside our own heads.</p>
        <p>The fear carries over too. In 2014, psychologists at the University of Virginia asked people to sit alone with their thoughts for fifteen minutes, with no phone and no book. The room held a button that delivered a mild electric shock. A majority of the men and about a quarter of the women pressed it. They chose pain over an unlit mind.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">What the Dark Gives Back</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Keeping everything bright costs us five things.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Our body clocks.</strong> Evening light holds back melatonin, the hormone that signals sleep, and pushes the body's internal clock later. In 2013 the researcher Kenneth Wright sent people camping for a week with nothing but sunlight and firelight, and their internal clocks moved roughly two hours earlier, back in line with the sun. What we call our natural rhythm is partly an artifact of the bulb.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>A whole way of seeing.</strong> Our eyes take twenty to thirty minutes to adapt fully to darkness, and most of us never wait that long. We carry a visual system built for the night that we almost never switch on.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The sky.</strong> According to a 2016 world atlas of light pollution, more than a third of humanity, and roughly eight in ten North Americans, can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live. For every generation before ours it was simply there, overhead, on any clear night.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The rest of the living world.</strong> Night lighting disorients migrating birds, devastates insects and leads hatchling sea turtles inland instead of out to sea.</p>
        <p><strong>The quiet hour.</strong> This is the one I keep coming back to. The space Ekirch found in those old diaries, the hour of lying awake and thinking, did not vanish because people stopped needing it. It vanished because we filled it. Night vision needs twenty minutes to arrive, and I suspect the mind works the same way. Whatever shows up in the dark needs time to adjust, and we never give it that time.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Case for the Switch</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">It would be dishonest to pretend light is the villain here. It is not.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Light keeps older people from falling on the stairs. It lets children do homework and parents work the second shift. It carries people through long northern winters and seasonal depression. Cheap light helped make mass literacy possible, and it opened the night to people who had been shut out of it. For some people a dark, quiet room is not peaceful at all. It is the worst part of the day.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">So the argument is not against light. It is against the default. There is a difference between choosing a lamp and obeying a reflex. A warm, low light in the one corner where you are reading is a decision. Every room blazing because the sun went down is a habit we inherited and never checked.</p>
        <p>The same goes for the mind. Information is good, and connection is good. Constant input that we never chose is something else.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Luxury, Convenience, Obligation</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The lightbulb follows a pattern that shows up again and again. First a thing is a luxury: rare, expensive, special. Then it becomes a convenience: cheap, everywhere, nice to have. Then it hardens into an obligation: expected, required, written into the code, and noticed only when it breaks.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Air conditioning did this. Phones did it. Being reachable at all hours did it. Each arrived as a gift and stayed as a job.</p>
        <p>The useful question is not whether any one of them is good or bad. It is simpler and harder: which of my defaults did I actually choose?</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">One Evening</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Here is the experiment. One evening this week, when the light starts to go, do not reach for the wall. Let the room go blue, then gray, then dark. Put the phone in another room. Sit for twenty minutes, long enough for your eyes to adjust, and maybe your mind too.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Notice what comes back.</p>
        <p>The sun went down tonight the way it always has. For the first time in a long time, I let it.</p>
      </div>
      <EssayFooter slug="the-switch-we-never-chose" />

    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
